  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — sings is an action verb and needs an adverb of manner. Beautiful is an adjective, beauty a noun and beautify a verb.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — taste is a linking verb here, so the word describes the soup and must be an adjective: good. Well is the adverb, goodly is archaic, and better than needs a second term.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the blank describes how he drives, so an adverb is required: carefully. Careful is an adjective, care and carefulness are nouns.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — two things take the comparative better. Best would need three or more; most good doubles the comparison; goodest is not a word.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — in the class compares her with all the students, so the superlative tallest is required. Taller compares two; most tall and more tall double the comparison.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — better is already the comparative of good, so it stands alone. A doubles it with more, C doubles it with most, and D makes a double comparison out of a one-syllable adjective.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A — a two-syllable adjective ending in -y changes the y to i and adds -er: happier. More happy is redundant, happyer misspells it, and most happy is the superlative.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — two syllables or more take more: more careful. Carefuller and carefulest are not English, and most careful is the superlative.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — good has the irregular forms good, better, best. Goodest and most good double the comparison, and better is the comparative.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — hard is itself the adverb meaning with effort. Hardly means almost not, which contradicts passing; harder and hardest compare him with others, which the sentence does not do.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — lately means recently, which is what the sentence needs. Late means after the expected time, later is comparative, and latest is superlative.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — nearly means almost, which is the sense required. Near means close in distance, and nearer and nearest are comparative forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — highly means very much. High refers to physical height, and higher and highest are comparisons of height.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the opening phrase must be followed by the person who was walking. In A, C and D the rain or it is made to walk.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the phrase in paper cups describes the tea, so it belongs next to tea. A makes the guests sit in cups, C strangles the sentence, and D separates the verb from its object.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — only sits immediately before Chidi, so it says one person passed. Moving it after the verb would say that passing was all he did.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — a clock time is a point, so at; a day takes on. In marks an enclosure or a period, which neither blank needs.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — arrive in takes a city or a country; arrive at takes a building or a point. At would need a specific place such as the airport, on is used for surfaces, and to does not follow arrive.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — married to is the fixed pair. With, for and by are translations rather than English collocations here.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — depend on is the fixed pair. Of, to and for do not collocate with depend in this sense.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A — consist of is the fixed pair. With, in and from change the meaning or produce a phrase that English does not use.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — comply with is the fixed pair. To, of and for are common translation errors.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — prefer takes to when two things are named. Than follows a comparative adjective, over is American and informal here, and from belongs to different.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — afraid of is the fixed pair. From, for and with do not collocate with afraid.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — good at names a skill. Good in would name a place or a field of activity, and on and for do not collocate with good in this sense.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — however is a conjunctive adverb, so it needs a semicolon before it and a comma after. A is a splice, C misplaces the comma, and D keeps the splice.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — because is sufficient on its own. A and C add a second connective, and D joins a dependent clause to a main clause with and.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — although carries the contrast by itself. But and yet double it, and the fourth option removes the main clause's subject.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the second clause contrasts with the first, so yet or but is required. So would make the low pay a consequence of hard work, because would reverse the logic, and therefore needs a semicolon before it.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — a clause follows, so a conjunction is required: although. Despite and in spite of take a noun or a gerund, and however cannot introduce a dependent clause.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the blank describes how she spoke, so an adverb is required. Confident is an adjective, confidence a noun and confide a verb.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — were is a linking verb, so the word describes the students and must be an adjective: anxious. Anxiously is an adverb, and the other two are nouns.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — than marks a comparison of two, so the comparative faster is required. Fast is positive, fastest is superlative, and more fast doubles the comparison.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A — of all marks a comparison of more than two, so the superlative with the definite article is required. More diligent compares two, and most diligentest doubles the superlative.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — bottles are countable, so fewer is required. Less is for uncountable quantities, lesser means inferior, and little is positive rather than comparative.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — water is uncountable, so less is the comparative. Fewer is for countable nouns, lesser means inferior, and many is positive and countable.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — scarcely is followed by when. Than belongs to no sooner, then is an adverb of time, and that does not follow scarcely in this construction.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — items in a list must share the same form, and the gerund is used throughout. The others mix gerunds with infinitives or with bare forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — a noun phrase follows, so a preposition is required: in spite of. Although needs a clause, because would reverse the logic, and however cannot introduce a phrase.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — as … as is the equative comparison, so the second as is required. Than follows a comparative, like introduces a simile, and so belongs to so … that.
  ]

]

